{
  "annotation": "A functional description attached to a genomic feature — a gene name, protein function, GO term, or database cross-reference. Most are added by analysis tools such as BLAST or InterProScan.",
  "annotation type": "A category of annotation, grouped by where it comes from — for example Gene Ontology terms, protein domains, or BLAST homology descriptions. You choose which types a search looks inside.",
  "feature": "A sequence in the genome — a gene, mRNA, CDS, or protein. Annotations are attached to features.",
  "gene set": "A named, versioned collection of gene models for one assembly. An assembly can carry more than one gene set as its annotation is revised.",
  "assembly": "A specific genome-sequence build for an organism, identified by an accession (for example GCA_004027475.1).",
  "GO term": "A Gene Ontology term — a standardized label for a gene product's molecular function, biological process, or cellular location (for example GO:0006351).",
  "mRNA": "Messenger RNA — a gene's spliced transcript: exons joined, introns removed, including the untranslated regions (UTRs).",
  "transcript": "An RNA copied from a gene. On this site a gene's transcripts are its mRNAs: a gene with several isoforms has several transcripts, each with its own exons, CDS and protein.",
  "isoform": "One of several transcripts from the same gene, differing in which exons they keep (alternative splicing) or where they start or end. Each isoform can give a different protein.",
  "exon": "A part of a gene kept in the spliced mRNA. Exons hold the coding sequence and the untranslated regions (UTRs).",
  "intron": "A part of a gene copied into RNA and then spliced out, so it is absent from the mRNA, the CDS and the protein. It is still in the genomic region.",
  "UTR": "Untranslated region — the stretch of an mRNA before the start codon (5′ UTR) or after the stop codon (3′ UTR). Part of the transcript, but not of the CDS or protein.",
  "gene model": "The predicted structure of a gene in an assembly: where its exons, introns, CDS and UTRs lie. Gene models come from an annotation pipeline, so they can be wrong or incomplete.",
  "strand": "Which of the two DNA strands a feature is read from: plus (+) runs left to right along the assembly, minus (−) right to left. A minus-strand sequence is given reverse-complemented, so it reads 5′ to 3′.",
  "CDS": "Coding sequence — the part of an mRNA that is translated into protein, without the untranslated regions.",
  "protein": "The amino-acid sequence translated from a feature's coding sequence (CDS).",
  "Genomic region": "The stretch of genome a gene or transcript occupies, introns included, read 5′ to 3′ along it (a minus-strand region is reverse-complemented). Upstream and downstream flanks are the bases just before its start and just after its end.",
  "contig": "A continuous stretch of assembled sequence with no gaps.",
  "scaffold": "Contigs ordered and joined into a longer sequence, with gaps (runs of N) where the sequence between them is unknown. Many assemblies are scaffolds rather than whole chromosomes.",
  "transcriptome": "An assembly of an organism's RNA instead of its genome: transcripts with no introns and no genomic position. Searches and downloads work, but flanks and genomic regions do not exist.",
  "BUSCO": "A completeness check. It counts how many genes expected in nearly every species of a lineage are found in an assembly or gene set: complete, duplicated, fragmented or missing. A high complete score means little is missing.",
  "gene": "A stretch of DNA that encodes a functional product. A gene is transcribed into one or more RNAs: mRNAs for protein-coding genes, which can be translated into proteins, and RNAs that are never translated, such as tRNAs, rRNAs and lncRNAs, for the rest.",
  "accession": "The stable identifier a database gives a record — a gene, protein, assembly or domain — such as GCF_000001405.40 or PF00069. Quoting the accession finds exactly that record.",
  "FASTA": "A plain-text sequence format: a line starting with > holding the name, then the sequence. What BLAST takes and what sequence downloads give you.",
  "GFF": "A tab-separated file of where features lie on an assembly — genes, mRNAs, exons, CDS — with their coordinates, strand and parent. The gene models on this site come from one.",
  "JBrowse track": "One layer of data in the genome browser, drawn along the assembly: gene models, RNA-seq coverage, repeats and so on. Tracks can be switched on and off.",
  "PANTHER": "A database that sorts proteins into families and subfamilies by their evolutionary relationships, then assigns likely functions from that classification. Here it appears as the source of family and Gene Ontology annotations.",
  "ortholog": "Genes in different species that descend from one gene in their last common ancestor — separated by speciation, not by duplication. Orthologs usually keep the same function, which is why a gene's name and description are often taken from its ortholog in a well-studied species. Copies made by a duplication within one lineage are paralogs.",
  "paralog": "Genes related by a duplication rather than by speciation — copies of one ancestral gene that sit side by side in the same lineage, often in the same genome. Once duplicated, a copy is free to change, so paralogs can take on new or narrower functions; a family of paralogs is why one gene can have several close matches in another species.",
  "homolog": "Any genes sharing a common ancestor, whether separated by speciation (orthologs) or by duplication (paralogs). Similar sequence suggests homology; it does not say which kind.",
  "OMA": "Orthologous MAtrix, a database of orthologs and paralogs across hundreds of genomes. It reports them as pairwise orthologs (one gene matched to one in another species), groups (genes that are all orthologs of one another), and HOGs — hierarchical orthologous groups, all the genes descended from one ancestral gene, so they hold paralogs too.",
  "RBBH": "Reciprocal Best BLAST Hit — two sequences in different species that each find the other as their single best match. It is a conservative way to infer that a pair are orthologs rather than merely similar.",
  "query": "The sequence you search with. Each sequence it finds in the database is a hit (also called the subject).",
  "E-value": "How many hits this good you would expect by chance in a database this size. Smaller is better: 1e-30 is almost certainly real, 1 is noise. It grows with the database, and a very short query cannot reach a small one.",
  "bit score": "A score for an alignment that does not depend on the database size, so it compares across searches. Higher is better.",
  "percent identity": "The share of aligned positions where the two sequences are identical. It covers only the aligned part, so 100% over 20 letters of a 2,000-letter query is still a partial match — check coverage too.",
  "coverage": "How much of a sequence the alignment spans. Query coverage of 30% means the hit matches only a third of your query.",
  "amplicon": "The stretch of DNA a primer pair copies in PCR, from the start of one primer to the end of the other. Its length is the product size.",
  "Tm": "Melting temperature — the temperature at which half of a primer is bound to its target. The two primers of a pair should have similar Tm, usually 57–63 °C.",
  "GC content": "The share of a sequence made of G and C. G–C pairs bind more strongly than A–T, so GC content raises Tm; primers usually aim for 40–60%.",
  "EggNOG": "A database of orthologous groups built across thousands of genomes. A new sequence inherits functional descriptions from the group it falls into.",
  "InterPro": "A service that combines the signatures of several member databases — PANTHER and Pfam among them — into a single classification, so a protein is described once rather than once per database.",
  "Pfam": "A collection of protein families, each defined by a statistical profile built from an alignment of known members. A match means the region belongs to that family.",
  "UniProt": "A central database of protein sequences and their functions. Its manually reviewed section is Swiss-Prot; the much larger automatically generated section is TrEMBL.",
  "Swiss-Prot": "The manually reviewed section of UniProt. Entries have been read and curated by a person, which makes them higher-confidence than automatically generated records.",
  "ProtNLM": "A machine-learning model that proposes a protein's name from its sequence alone. These names are predictions rather than curated assignments, and are best treated as a starting point.",
  "InterProScan": "The tool that runs a protein against InterPro's member databases — PANTHER, Pfam, SMART and others — in one pass. The member that made a given call is named in brackets after it.",
  "Ensembl": "A genome annotation project and database covering many species. Here it names the species a comparison was run against, not the annotation itself.",
  "UniProtKB": "The UniProt Knowledgebase: the combined protein database whose manually reviewed section is Swiss-Prot and whose automatically generated section is TrEMBL."
}
